🚀 *** What is @SpringBootApplication ? ***

It’s a meta-annotation in Spring Boot — meaning it combines several commonly used annotations into one.

When you put it on your main class, like:

``java

@SpringBootApplication

public class MyApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(MyApplication.class, args);
    }
}
``

Spring Boot automatically configures, scans, and initializes your app.

That means it is equivalent to writing three annotations together:

@SpringBootConfiguration
@EnableAutoConfiguration
@ComponentScan

1️⃣ ***@SpringBootConfiguration***


A specialized form of @Configuration.

Marks this class as a source of bean definitions.

It tells Spring: “You can use this class to register beans with the application context.”

2️⃣ ***@EnableAutoConfiguration***

The “magic” part of Spring Boot ✨

Tells Spring Boot to automatically configure beans based on:

What’s on the classpath (e.g. spring-boot-starter-web → configures Tomcat + DispatcherServlet)

Existing beans (so it doesn’t override your custom ones)

Property settings (from application.properties)

Example:
If you add spring-boot-starter-data-jpa, Spring Boot will:

Create a DataSource

Configure EntityManagerFactory

Set up JpaRepositories automatically

3️⃣ ***@ComponentScan***

Scans the current package (and sub-packages) for Spring components:

@Component

@Service

@Repository

@Controller

@RestController

@Configuration

That’s why your main class should usually be in the root package, so Spring can discover all sub-packages.